  "summary": "Matched-budget and protocol-matched ADMET benchmarks now isolate acquisition policy, model family and representation choice as the variables under study.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is benchmark design as the object of study in ADMET prediction. The active-learning benchmark compares seven acquisition strategies and two uncertainty methods across eight regression endpoints under a strict protocol. The GNN study uses a matched-budget, scaffold-split design across six TDC endpoints, comparing GNNs, frozen pretrained models (ChemBERTa, MolCLR) and fingerprints. Ark-ADMET shows that endpoint-specific selection of fingerprints, descriptors, GIN/MiniMol embeddings and CatBoost hyperparameters matches or beats leaderboard scores on 7 of 21 endpoints. What is new is controlled comparison of conventional pipeline decisions under matched budgets and split protocols, rather than new architectures. Adjacent items on single-cell foundation models and antibody immunogenicity likewise test whether learned predictors outperform simple baselines, though they do not share the ADMET setting.",
